From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Haapanen <ian.haapanen@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 12:56:36 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] x86/knc: /dev/phiblk0, a block device served by the host over
 ring channel 4

The host tool serves a disk image to the card through a fourth ring
channel: the card posts 32-byte request records (tag, operation, byte
length, sector, card physical address) and the host answers with 8-byte
completions; an identify record returns the capacity. A blk-mq driver
with one hardware queue turns the channel into /dev/phiblk0; a kernel
thread polls the completion ring, spinning briefly after activity and
sleeping 200 us at a time when idle, and registers the disk from a
workqueue once identify is answered (add_disk reads the device, so it
cannot run on the poller).

Two data paths. In direct mode the request's pages are handed to the
host, one record per physical segment, with no copy on the card. In
bounce mode data goes through 512 KiB uncached slots in the channel's
data area, part of the ring region, with one record per request and the
request tag as the slot number. The host chooses through the tag of its
identify answer: 0xfffffffe when its path into card memory is coherent
with the cores' caches (the DMA engine), 0xffffffff when it is not (writes
through the PCIe aperture, which a page the card had just written did not
see; measured 2026-09-16, both memory zones). knc_blk.direct=0 or 1
overrides the choice.

The poller resynchronises on an impossible byte count, the guard the
other channels carry: the host's index writes went out byte by byte until
fixed in the host tool, and a torn head produced floods of zero
completions.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/x86/include/asm/knc_ring.h |   3 +
 arch/x86/kernel/Makefile        |   2 +-
 arch/x86/kernel/knc_blk.c       | 448 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 3 files changed, 452 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)
 create mode 100644 arch/x86/kernel/knc_blk.c

